## 1. Alva permanently auto-locks itself to landscape

### Current behaviour
- `manifest.webmanifest` already declares `"orientation": "landscape"`, but that only works for an installed PWA on some platforms.
- `js/core/device.js` sets `portrait = h > w * 1.05` and `blockedPortrait = touchUI && portrait`, and toggles `html.is-portrait-blocked`. That class shows the `.rotate-overlay` in `index.html` ("Rotate your device / Alva is designed for landscape play", styled in `css/overlays.css`, strings `rotate.title` / `rotate.text` in `js/localization/strings/en.js` + `fr.js`).
- Nothing in `js/` calls `screen.orientation.lock`, so the game never rotates itself. It only asks the player to rotate.

### Wanted
On touch devices Alva locks itself to landscape automatically, keeps the lock for the whole session, and puts it back whenever it is lost.

Implementation:
1. Create a small module, e.g. `js/core/orientation-lock.js`, or add the logic to `Device` in `js/core/device.js`, whichever fits the existing structure better. It should:
   - Call `screen.orientation.lock('landscape')` when the API exists (feature-detect it, and catch the rejected promise quietly because it rejects on unsupported browsers and outside fullscreen).
   - Most browsers only allow `lock()` in fullscreen or in an installed app (`display-mode: standalone/fullscreen`, see `APP_DISPLAY_MODES` in `js/core/web-app-install.js`). On touch UI, on the first user gesture (`pointerdown` / `touchend` / `keydown`, once), call `document.documentElement.requestFullscreen({ navigationUI: 'hide' })` (with the webkit prefix fallback), then lock. Don't request fullscreen on desktop / non-touch devices, and don't lock there.
   - Lock again after `fullscreenchange`, `visibilitychange` (back to visible), `orientationchange` / `screen.orientation` `change`, and app resume. "Permanently" means the game keeps restoring the lock. There's no setting to turn it off.
   - Never throw. On iOS Safari, which has no `lock()`, fall back silently.
2. Keep the rotate overlay (`is-portrait-blocked`) as the **fallback only**, for when the lock is unavailable or was refused. When the lock succeeds, the player should never see the overlay. The existing `blockedPortrait` logic in `device.js` can stay as-is, since a successful lock keeps the window landscape.
3. Keep `"orientation": "landscape"` in the manifest. Also check that `index.html` has no conflicting meta tags.
4. Make sure nothing else breaks: the touch layout editor (`js/ui/touch-layout-editor.js`) and `touch-controls.js` re-place themselves on orientation/resize, and online play, the install dialog and the splash screen have to work while in fullscreen.
5. Tests: add tests (e.g. in `tests/interface/screen-guard.test.mjs` or a new `orientation-lock.test.mjs`) with a fake `screen.orientation` and fullscreen API covering: lock is requested on touch after the first gesture; lock is not requested on desktop; it is re-requested after `visibilitychange` / `fullscreenchange`; a rejected `lock()` or a missing API doesn't throw and leaves the rotate overlay as the fallback.

