## 3. Every spawn and respawn plays the elimination burst in reverse

### Current behaviour (the "ejected" effect)
- When a fighter is taken by the Void, `Arena.checkVoid` (`js/game/arena.js` ~line 340) calls `this.fx.addElimination(f)`.
- `HitEffects.addElimination` / `drawEliminations` in `js/game/rendering/hit-fx.js` (~lines 289 and 676), tuned by `HIT_FX.elimination` (life 0.55 s, 24 shards, radius 0.95, shardLength 0.2, ring 0.85, shake 5, reducedTravel 0.3). The burst has a white flash that pops and shrinks, a ring opening outward in the fighter's first palette colour, and shards in the fighter's `visual.eliminationPalette` (or `NEUTRAL_ELIMINATION_PALETTE`) flying outward with an ease-out, each with a thin dark under-stroke, all fading out. The shards are seeded, so the same burst always draws the same shards.
- Spawns happen at: match start and `Battle.restart()` (`js/game/battle.js`, `f.reset(this.stage)`, followed by `this.fx.reset()`, so any effect added before that reset is wiped); respawn in `Arena.updateRespawns` (`f.respawn(this.stage)`); and Practice Ground creating or replacing fighters (`js/game/practice.js` ~lines 72–110, also calls `this.fx.reset()`), plus its respawns. Check online (`js/online/`), Local Play, Quick Battle, Watch mode and rematch paths too: they should all go through these, but verify it.

### Wanted
Each time a fighter appears (initial spawn at match start, every restart/rematch, every respawn after the Void, every Practice Ground fighter spawn or swap), play the **same elimination burst run backwards** at the fighter's spawn position:
- The shards start at their full outward reach and fly **inward** to the fighter's centre (the time-reverse of the ease-out, so they accelerate in, the same curve as `out` evaluated at `1 - k`).
- The ring starts at its widest (`cfg.ring` fighter heights) and **closes** to its smallest.
- Things fade **in** instead of out, and the white flash grows and pops at the **end**, as the fighter "materialises".
- It uses the same palette (`eliminationPalette(f.def)`), the same size (`f.def.visual.height ?? body.height`), and is centred on `body.x, body.y - body.height / 2` at the spawn point, with a deterministic seed.
- The same shake (none with reduced motion), and the same reduced-motion handling (`reducedTravel`).

Implementation guidance:
1. Generalise rather than duplicate: give elimination entries a `reverse` flag (or add `addSpawn(f)` that pushes into the same list with `reverse: true`), and in `drawEliminations` map `k → 1 - k` for the reversed ones, so it really is the same drawing played backwards. Keep `HIT_FX.elimination` as the single source of tuning. If a separate knob is needed (e.g. its own `life`), add `HIT_FX.spawn` next to it with a comment saying it mirrors elimination.
2. Shared code only: never special-case a fighter id (`CLAUDE.md`). Each fighter's look comes from its existing `visual.eliminationPalette` character data.
3. Call it from the spawn points:
   - `Arena.updateRespawns`: right after `f.respawn(this.stage)`.
   - `Battle.restart()` and Practice Ground's spawn/swap: **after** `this.fx.reset()`, otherwise the reset clears it. Put a helper on `Arena`, e.g. `announceSpawn(f)`, and use it everywhere.
4. **Paint only.** It must not change the fighter's state, physics, hitboxes, timers, input, CPU decisions, score or match stats. The fighter is in play and controllable exactly as it is now (no added invulnerability or delay). Online lockstep determinism (`tests/online/determinism.test.mjs`, `lockstep.test.mjs`) must still pass, since fx is not part of simulated state. Check that, and that the celebration test which counts `addElimination` calls (`tests/systems/celebration.test.mjs:329`) still counts only eliminations. If the spawn burst shares the function, keep a separate entry point so that count isn't polluted.
5. Draw order: in `Arena` render (~line 506, `drawEliminations`), the reversed burst draws over the fighter so it covers the appearance.
6. Tests (extend `tests/systems/hit-fx.test.mjs`, `void-ripple.test.mjs` or add `spawn-burst.test.mjs`):
   - A spawn burst is added on match start, on restart (and still present after `fx.reset()`), on every respawn, and on Practice Ground spawn/swap.
   - It uses the fighter's palette and size, and it expires after its life.
   - It is the elimination run backwards: at age `t` the reversed burst draws the same geometry as the elimination at age `life - t` (compare with a recording fake ctx, like the `overtime.test.mjs` draw tests).
   - Reduced motion: no shake, short travel.
   - A battle simulated with and without the spawn burst produces identical simulation state (paint only).

